  "_what_this_is": "The bytes of the program the test RUNNER executes. ROUND 68, THE CHALLENGER'S R62-F-2 (P1): every arm of this family defends the law's process by reading FLAGS, and none of them read the runner's own program -- one import inserted into node_modules/vitest/vitest.mjs ran inside the gate's own run, which printed its full census and exited 0. This file is the pin, read independently by test/tool-commands-are-isolated.test.ts and by tools/law-ran.py. It moves in the commit that moves package-lock.json, and `version`/`lockfile_integrity` are compared against the lockfile so that a dependency bump without a re-pin refuses by name rather than passing.",
  "_what_it_does_not_cover": "THIS PACKAGE ONLY, one hop deep, and the figure printed by the gate says so in those words. vitest imports vite, tinypool, chai and the rest of the installed tree IN THE SAME PROCESS; those files are NOT pinned here, and the measurement that says so is the attacker's own import placed in node_modules/vite/dist/node/index.js, which ran TWICE with the suite 33/33 green at rc 0 -- and, as the coordinator's R68-C-01 measured on the signed head, twice inside the GATE's own run at rc 0 with the full census printed. A list of names also has the enumeration's time hole at the PACKAGE boundary: a file arriving inside this package refuses, a whole package arriving beside it was never named. The pin narrows the attacker from 'anyone who can write under node_modules' to 'anyone who can write under node_modules outside this one package', and nothing more. Closing the class means deriving the set from EXECUTION -- what this run actually loaded -- rather than enumerating a tree, and the figures that sentence weighed are in `_how_the_tree_figures_were_counted` below, with the command and the rule that produced them. ",
